    {
      "id": "7usPriXoTQI-0X_Z3ywr2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2061.825093971359,
      "y": -54.06725794456514,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "KtQIfG8n4fBmv6gQHGQn_",
        "PUd7chwUJnvQPN3OpsLAA",
        "lEpZNnZ2iWhntdpU33pqF",
        "NLJJtaC2L4CFMmspClZu3",
        "C7FE7kMtKvCHAv0gUnl7L"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0U",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1884627451,
      "version": 7868,
      "versionNonce": 1124959373,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791465568297,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "it is a substantial refinement of SPLADE that improves the effectiveness–efficiency trade-off of\nlearned sparse retrieval.\n\n\nThe paper introduces three main improvements:\n\n- Max pooling instead of sum pooling: Original SPLADE aggregates token-level vocabulary activations by\nsumming them. SPLADE v2 replaces this with max pooling, keeping the strongest activation for each\nvocabulary term across the sequence. This simple change produces a clear effectiveness gain and\nbecomes the default configuration, called SPLADE-max.     SPLADE v2: Sparse Lexical and E…\n\n- A document-only variant: SPLADE-doc\n  SPLADE v2 introduces a version where only documents are expanded and reweighted, while queries are\nleft unexpanded. This is important for efficiency because document representations can be precomputed\noffline and indexed, reducing query-time latency while still maintaining competitive retrieval quality.\nSPLADE v2: Sparse Lexical and E…\n\n- Knowledge distillation and harder negatives\n  The authors add a distillation-based training procedure, using a stronger reranker as a teacher and\nharder negative examples. This produces DistilSPLADE-max, which significantly improves ranking\nperformance over the original SPLADE.     SPLADE v2: Sparse Lexical and E…\n\n\n\nIn SPLADE v2, the sum is replaced by a maximum:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nImagine the document: “The doctor prescribed medicine”\n\nFor the vocabulary term treatment, different input tokens might activate it with different strengths:\n\n                                        Input token       score for \"treatment\"\n                                        \n                                        doctor                 1.2\n                                        prescribed             2.8\n                                        medicine               3.6\n                                        the                     0.1\n\nWith sum pooling: 1.2 + 2.8 + 3.6 + 0.1 = 7.7\n\nWith max pooling: max(1.2,2.8,3.6,0.1) = 3.6\n\nFor each vocabulary concept, keep the strongest evidence anywhere in the sequence, rather than\naccumulating evidence from every token.\n\nThis matters because several contextualized tokens can all weakly predict the same vocabulary term.\nWith sum pooling, those weak activations can accumulate and make the term look disproportionately\nimportant. With max pooling, the representation instead reflects the single strongest contextual signal\nfor that term.\n\nIn SPLADE v2, distillation means that SPLADE is trained not only from binary relevance labels, but also\nto imitate the ranking preferences of a stronger cross-encoder teacher. The paper introduces this\ntogether with hard-negative mining.     \n\nThe distillation training is done in two steps: \n    (1) we first train both a SPLADE first-stage retriever as well as a cross-encoder reranker \n    (2) in the second step, generate triplets using SPLADE trained with distillation (thus providing\n        harder negatives than BM25), and use the aforementioned reranker to generate the scores\n        needed for the Margin-MSE loss. The result of the second step is what we call DistilSPLADE\n        max.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n, on MS MARCO and TREC, max pooling brings almost 2 points in MRR@10 and NDCG@10 compared to\nthe SPLADE baseline.\n\nOur document encoder model with max pooling is able to reach the same performance as the previous\nSPLADE model, outperforming doc2query-T5 on MS MARCO. As this model has no query encoder, it is\nmore efficient in terms of e.g. latency.\n\nBy training with distillation, we are able to considerably improve the performance of SPLADE, as seen in\nwe observe that distilled models bring huge improvements for higher values of FLOPS (0.368 MRR@10\nfor ≈ 4 FLOPS), but are still very efficient in low regime (0.35 MRR for ≈ 0.3 FLOPS).",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "it is a substantial refinement of SPLADE that improves the effectiveness–efficiency trade-off of learned sparse retrieval.\n\n\nThe paper introduces three main improvements:\n\n- Max pooling instead of sum pooling: Original SPLADE aggregates token-level vocabulary activations by summing them. SPLADE v2 replaces this with max pooling, keeping the strongest activation for each vocabulary term across the sequence. This simple change produces a clear effectiveness gain and becomes the default configuration, called SPLADE-max.     SPLADE v2: Sparse Lexical and E…\n\n- A document-only variant: SPLADE-doc\n  SPLADE v2 introduces a version where only documents are expanded and reweighted, while queries are left unexpanded. This is important for efficiency because document representations can be precomputed offline and indexed, reducing query-time latency while still maintaining competitive retrieval quality.     SPLADE v2: Sparse Lexical and E…\n\n- Knowledge distillation and harder negatives\n  The authors add a distillation-based training procedure, using a stronger reranker as a teacher and harder negative examples. This produces DistilSPLADE-max, which significantly improves ranking performance over the original SPLADE.     SPLADE v2: Sparse Lexical and E…\n\n\n\nIn SPLADE v2, the sum is replaced by a maximum:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nImagine the document: “The doctor prescribed medicine”\n\nFor the vocabulary term treatment, different input tokens might activate it with different strengths:\n\n                                        Input token       score for \"treatment\"\n                                        \n                                        doctor                 1.2\n                                        prescribed             2.8\n                                        medicine               3.6\n                                        the                     0.1\n\nWith sum pooling: 1.2 + 2.8 + 3.6 + 0.1 = 7.7\n\nWith max pooling: max(1.2,2.8,3.6,0.1) = 3.6\n\nFor each vocabulary concept, keep the strongest evidence anywhere in the sequence, rather than accumulating evidence from every token.\n\nThis matters because several contextualized tokens can all weakly predict the same vocabulary term. With sum pooling, those weak activations can accumulate and make the term look disproportionately important. With max pooling, the representation instead reflects the single strongest contextual signal for that term.\n\nIn SPLADE v2, distillation means that SPLADE is trained not only from binary relevance labels, but also to imitate the ranking preferences of a stronger cross-encoder teacher. The paper introduces this together with hard-negative mining.     \n\nThe distillation training is done in two steps: \n    (1) we first train both a SPLADE first-stage retriever as well as a cross-encoder reranker \n    (2) in the second step, generate triplets using SPLADE trained with distillation (thus providing\n        harder negatives than BM25), and use the aforementioned reranker to generate the scores\n        needed for the Margin-MSE loss. The result of the second step is what we call DistilSPLADE\n        max.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n, on MS MARCO and TREC, max pooling brings almost 2 points in MRR@10 and NDCG@10 compared to the SPLADE baseline.\n\nOur document encoder model with max pooling is able to reach the same performance as the previous SPLADE model, outperforming doc2query-T5 on MS MARCO. As this model has no query encoder, it is more efficient in terms of e.g. latency.\n\nBy training with distillation, we are able to considerably improve the performance of SPLADE, as seen in we observe that distilled models bring huge improvements for higher values of FLOPS (0.368 MRR@10 for ≈ 4 FLOPS), but are still very efficient in low regime (0.35 MRR for ≈ 0.3 FLOPS).",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
