      "updated": 1791465021394,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SPLADE Training",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SPLADE Training",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "HmZoT40CJ0d8Bdlg7odAY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 850.6884252892601,
      "y": 3553.5939053060415,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "FkoSQXoTgtWOj8R4svBfq",
        "D4LWXpFB0SWYPRA17i-MO",
        "beGSV2IDcPx802ccbHRD9",
        "PV584OzRxW5NihzuVO3sS",
        "efZbnwvkwM6Iekez_vZ6j",
        "L79udJmpJP3V8lg3tTYVS",
        "3Sw5YReK1wE1VDWXhqelM",
        "5vtirC1GDv5GVjzGyCXJQ"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b118",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1443393557,
      "version": 7833,
      "versionNonce": 1071820493,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791465016925,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SPLADE is fine-tuned from a pretrained BERT-base model on a ranking dataset by adapting BERT from\na masked-language-model encoder into a sparse retriever. \n\nInstead of predicting a class label, the model takes a query or a document as input and produces a\nsparse weighted vector over the whole vocabulary. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe training dataset is MS MARCO Passage Ranking, containing approximately 8.8 million passages and\nhundreds of thousands of training queries.\nFor each query, the training procedure considers:\n\n        \\(q\\)        Training query\n        \\(d^+\\)        Relevant (positive) passage\n        \\(d^-\\)        Hard negative passage\n\nSPLADE uses a softmax contrastive loss with in-batch negatives.\n\n The model computes a score for each query-document pair using the dot product between their sparse\nvectors, and it is trained so that the relevant document receives a higher score than the negatives. \n\n\nThe ranking loss is:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model learns to increase the similarity of relevant query–document pairs and decrease similarity to\nnegatives.\n\nWithout regularization, SPLADE could activate too many vocabulary dimensions, making the inverted\nindex expensive to search. SPLADE applies sparsity regularization to both query and document\nrepresentations, encouraging most vocabulary dimensions to remain zero so retrieval stays efficient. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIf a vocabulary term has a large average weight across the batch, its activations receive a larger\ndownward pressure from regularization.\n\nThe overall objective therefore combines a ranking loss, which improves relevance, with a regularization\nterm, which controls efficiency. The model can independently regulate query and document\nrepresentations.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SPLADE is fine-tuned from a pretrained BERT-base model on a ranking dataset by adapting BERT from a masked-language-model encoder into a sparse retriever. \n\nInstead of predicting a class label, the model takes a query or a document as input and produces a sparse weighted vector over the whole vocabulary. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe training dataset is MS MARCO Passage Ranking, containing approximately 8.8 million passages and hundreds of thousands of training queries.\nFor each query, the training procedure considers:\n\n        \\(q\\)        Training query\n        \\(d^+\\)        Relevant (positive) passage\n        \\(d^-\\)        Hard negative passage\n\nSPLADE uses a softmax contrastive loss with in-batch negatives.\n\n The model computes a score for each query-document pair using the dot product between their sparse vectors, and it is trained so that the relevant document receives a higher score than the negatives. \n\n\nThe ranking loss is:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model learns to increase the similarity of relevant query–document pairs and decrease similarity to negatives.\n\nWithout regularization, SPLADE could activate too many vocabulary dimensions, making the inverted index expensive to search. SPLADE applies sparsity regularization to both query and document representations, encouraging most vocabulary dimensions to remain zero so retrieval stays efficient. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIf a vocabulary term has a large average weight across the batch, its activations receive a larger downward pressure from regularization.\n\nThe overall objective therefore combines a ranking loss, which improves relevance, with a regularization term, which controls efficiency. The model can independently regulate query and document representations.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
