      "crop": null
    },
    {
      "id": "rIUFUnZWcDVnTatCYh7-7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2143.191555208101,
      "y": 4082.031685446036,
      "width": 589.5722045898438,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#6741d9",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 2,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "645IZE4hntfsWqUBcBwp7",
        "-fireszhhOnUeOklniCy2"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1x",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 270159052,
      "version": 106,
      "versionNonce": 996375412,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791537219187,
      "created": 1791531266577,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Approximate-nearest neighbor (ANN)",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Approximate-nearest neighbor (ANN)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "BG3DMNlpwzEqISVUbmR-N",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2375.9982672532387,
      "y": 4186.952121353297,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "V8Fid8JkOUNQyKry4DUF3",
        "TWtKViSX_z8xwGMYBZCdz",
        "X437_ECObPpsFUu6u-yE2",
        "GruMOUjNInZwRvvmURpCT",
        "xNDk09Rud8y885Lc0GrQt",
        "PJ_zw9wz0v0dM7tIPgAc6",
        "BwO3WEKBLMYbMwYtOP6Rv",
        "aZ7m-94_my9G-TUnEs6vs",
        "Id-LTNVJjxtYMUGL_h2s3",
        "-fireszhhOnUeOklniCy2"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1531892556,
      "version": 8489,
      "versionNonce": 97250548,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791537219187,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Vector databases are emerging as a core part of the AI tech stack, serving knowledge to large language\nmodels (LLMs).\n\nIn contrast to traditional search, vector search relies on vector similarity search techniques like k-\nnearest neighbor search (knn) to retrieve data points similar to a query vector based on some distance\nmetric.\n\nAlthough the distance metrics mentioned previously can be used to measure vector similarity, it becomes\ninefficient and slow to compare all possible vectors against the query vector at query time for similarity\nsearch. To solve for this, we can use an approximate-nearest neighbor (ANN) search.\n\nInstead of finding an exact match, ANN algorithms efficiently search for the vectors that are\napproximately closest to a given query. By allowing for some level of approximation, these algorithms can\nsignificantly reduce the computational cost of nearest neighbor search without the need to compute\nembedding similarities across an entire corpus.\n\nOne of the most popular ANN algorithms is Inverted File Index (IVF).\n\nThe core idea of an IVF index is to partition the vector space into clusters (often using an algorithm\nlike k-means) and then restrict searches to only the most relevant clusters.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe process begins with a coarse quantization step: a clustering algorithm (commonly k-means) is applied\nto the dataset to find a fixed number of cluster centroids. This number is typically denoted as nlist,\nrepresenting the number of clusters (or inverted lists) in the index. Each centroid is the representative\ncenter of a cluster in the vector space.\n\nAfter computing the centroids, every data vector in the dataset is assigned to its nearest centroid.\nFor each centroid (cluster), the index stores an inverted list containing the identifiers (and sometimes\nthe vectors) of all data points that belong to that cluster.\n\nOnce the IVF index is built, querying it involves a two-stage search pipeline that dramatically cuts down\nthe work needed for finding nearest neighbors:\n\n    1) Coarse Search (Centroid Retrieval): When a query vector is given, the first step is to identify the\n        clusters most likely to contain its nearest neighbors. The parameter nprobe controls how many\n        clusters will be searched – effectively how broad the search scope is.\n    2) Fine Search (Within Selected Clusters): After choosing the most relevant clusters, the search\n        continues by examining only the vectors within those clusters’ inverted lists. \n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Vector databases are emerging as a core part of the AI tech stack, serving knowledge to large language models (LLMs).\n\nIn contrast to traditional search, vector search relies on vector similarity search techniques like k-nearest neighbor search (knn) to retrieve data points similar to a query vector based on some distance metric.\n\nAlthough the distance metrics mentioned previously can be used to measure vector similarity, it becomes inefficient and slow to compare all possible vectors against the query vector at query time for similarity search. To solve for this, we can use an approximate-nearest neighbor (ANN) search.\n\nInstead of finding an exact match, ANN algorithms efficiently search for the vectors that are approximately closest to a given query. By allowing for some level of approximation, these algorithms can significantly reduce the computational cost of nearest neighbor search without the need to compute embedding similarities across an entire corpus.\n\nOne of the most popular ANN algorithms is Inverted File Index (IVF).\n\nThe core idea of an IVF index is to partition the vector space into clusters (often using an algorithm like k-means) and then restrict searches to only the most relevant clusters.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe process begins with a coarse quantization step: a clustering algorithm (commonly k-means) is applied to the dataset to find a fixed number of cluster centroids. This number is typically denoted as nlist, representing the number of clusters (or inverted lists) in the index. Each centroid is the representative center of a cluster in the vector space.\n\nAfter computing the centroids, every data vector in the dataset is assigned to its nearest centroid. For each centroid (cluster), the index stores an inverted list containing the identifiers (and sometimes the vectors) of all data points that belong to that cluster.\n\nOnce the IVF index is built, querying it involves a two-stage search pipeline that dramatically cuts down the work needed for finding nearest neighbors:\n\n    1) Coarse Search (Centroid Retrieval): When a query vector is given, the first step is to identify the\n        clusters most likely to contain its nearest neighbors. The parameter nprobe controls how many\n        clusters will be searched – effectively how broad the search scope is.\n    2) Fine Search (Within Selected Clusters): After choosing the most relevant clusters, the search\n        continues by examining only the vectors within those clusters’ inverted lists. \n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791536273489,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
