    {
      "id": "fV7C8geoXNX6kYeiHEGva",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2433.9446083471557,
      "y": 9170.891266901392,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "3tU2Ttl8333ZwaixzINIF",
        "VRLhXxrhuFMMNnd5KmOi3",
        "7g4XvOW8u-eaCQO5HuQxC",
        "-DvEv2k-m4OSP-RZlcpNJ",
        "D0N_bds8joTfl-NEE-pEZ",
        "7juzs1hsW9WvcpEn5VTQD",
        "QBKm3-975lQkUPar38l6w",
        "9klwRzURI9hHE9tVMNw0M",
        "Ne_IDKd5F5Fh7WmYG1aJg",
        "kcQNvM4lVd9TZEhidRH44"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b25",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1063440756,
      "version": 8567,
      "versionNonce": 807630156,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791573754757,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Effectiveness: How relevant are the retrieved documents?\nEfficiency: How quickly can the system retrieve them?\nResources: How many resources retrieval methods consume?\n\nThe experiments use 29 datasets and compare three retrieval models:\n\n    - Traditional sparse: BM25               Lexical term matching\n    - Learned sparse: SPLADE++ ED        Learned sparse term weights\n    - Dense: BGE-base-en-v1.5               Dense semantic embeddings\n\n\nDense retrieval is often more accurate, but not always\nDense retrieval has a strong effectiveness advantage on many of these benchmarks, but sparse methods\nremain competitive, and sometimes outperform dense embeddings.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBM25 vs. Dense throughput\nAcross these six examples, BM25 processes approximately 4–10 times as many queries per second as\ndense retrieval.\nWithout online inference, the search latency of document-only mode is comparable to BM25.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSparse encoding results in a much smaller index size than dense encoding. A document-only sparse\nencoder generates an index that is 10.4% of the size of a dense encoding index. For a bi-encoder, the\nindex size is 7.2% of the size of a dense encoding index.\nDense encoding uses k-NN retrieval and incurs a 7.9% increase in RAM cost at search time. Neural sparse\nsearch uses a native Lucene index, so the RAM cost does not increase at search time.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Effectiveness: How relevant are the retrieved documents?\nEfficiency: How quickly can the system retrieve them?\nResources: How many resources retrieval methods consume?\n\nThe experiments use 29 datasets and compare three retrieval models:\n\n    - Traditional sparse: BM25               Lexical term matching\n    - Learned sparse: SPLADE++ ED        Learned sparse term weights\n    - Dense: BGE-base-en-v1.5               Dense semantic embeddings\n\n\nDense retrieval is often more accurate, but not always\nDense retrieval has a strong effectiveness advantage on many of these benchmarks, but sparse methods remain competitive, and sometimes outperform dense embeddings.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBM25 vs. Dense throughput\nAcross these six examples, BM25 processes approximately 4–10 times as many queries per second as dense retrieval.\nWithout online inference, the search latency of document-only mode is comparable to BM25.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSparse encoding results in a much smaller index size than dense encoding. A document-only sparse encoder generates an index that is 10.4% of the size of a dense encoding index. For a bi-encoder, the index size is 7.2% of the size of a dense encoding index.\nDense encoding uses k-NN retrieval and incurs a 7.9% increase in RAM cost at search time. Neural sparse search uses a native Lucene index, so the RAM cost does not increase at search time.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791570297876,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
