      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791573754757,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Query Expansion",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Query Expansion",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "created": 1791468069863,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "A2_9p-4hzj_9rB7jgQipi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2452.5536980862053,
      "y": 11484.402431902712,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "xo_Dd8bkx1_wvKNolO0iL",
        "cs4DIelxL7KW2wfvj8FnJ",
        "pb8G5Q126AtkKTfp1qMUB",
        "iW2UVK6if_T5eQuFGAU86",
        "6cpfkI3zckuKWT0S7xkrU",
        "gsXIg8RRKiGw5FPEjsWAz",
        "WfOs8fmxbh49Xy484WhXe",
        "wdUvzoXIlOSIIeolSTxpG",
        "QhMpTUIcvrbQfmX-brPiw"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 108800628,
      "version": 8568,
      "versionNonce": 1557531724,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791573754757,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The classic tf-idf or BM25 algorithms for IR have long been known to have a conceptual flaw: they\nwork only if there is exact overlap of words between the query and document, an issue called\nthe vocabulary mismatch problem.\n\nOne common practice of systematic reviewers is to manually create a huge fanout of search terms. This\nmakes sure every possible permutation is included.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nQuery expansion, then, is just automating this process. A generic language model can be prompted to\nspecify all the variations on each search term, and those can be fed into a regular full-text search.\n\nThis method is transparent and understandable, as the intermediate step is directly legible to humans.\nThe downside is that it’s slow, and the search results are generally still much worse than a vector\nsearch.\n\nSPLADE is similar to query expansion—in that the user’s query is enriched with synonymous or related\nterms—but rather than this expansion being a manual process, we instead lean on language models to\nsuggest additional search terms.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The classic tf-idf or BM25 algorithms for IR have long been known to have a conceptual flaw: they work only if there is exact overlap of words between the query and document, an issue called\nthe vocabulary mismatch problem.\n\nOne common practice of systematic reviewers is to manually create a huge fanout of search terms. This makes sure every possible permutation is included.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nQuery expansion, then, is just automating this process. A generic language model can be prompted to specify all the variations on each search term, and those can be fed into a regular full-text search.\n\nThis method is transparent and understandable, as the intermediate step is directly legible to humans. The downside is that it’s slow, and the search results are generally still much worse than a vector search.\n\nSPLADE is similar to query expansion—in that the user’s query is enriched with synonymous or related terms—but rather than this expansion being a manual process, we instead lean on language models to suggest additional search terms.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791571181585,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
